1,0$

1,0 $ Ascomata hypogeous, globose to subglobose, truffle-like $O. subterranean 4. 
1,2 $ Ascomata epigeous, cup-shaped to ear-shaped, split or entire
2,3 $ Spores ornamented
2,5 $ Spores smooth
3,0 $ Spores with fine warts ± ridges $ O. unicisa 17.
3,4 $ Spores spiny
4,0 $ Spores 14-17 × 7-9 µm $ O. kaushalii 16.
4,0 $ Spores 16.5-20 × 7.6-10 µm $ O. yunnanensis 18.
5,6 $ Spores Lm > 17 µm 
5,10 $ Spores Lm < 17 µm 
6,0 $ Apothecia broadly ear-shaped, split; typically ochraceous yellow to ochre orange; often associated with Cudonia in mossy Picea forests $ O. cantharella 11. 
6,7 $ Apothecia cup-shaped, split or entire; brown; under Picea or other trees
7,0 $ Apothecia entire; reddish brown; basal mycelium normally with abundant minute resinous exudates; ectal excipulum hyphoid hairs often with a gelatinous sheath (more easilyseen in MLZ); with Picea $ O. propinquata 12.
7,8 $ Apothecia split or entire; purple or ochre-brown; basal mycelium without or with sparse resinous exudates; ectal excipulum hyphoid hairs without a conspicuous sheath; with angiosperms
8,0 $ Apothecia split, up to 75 mm wide; spores broadly ellipsoid to oblong, Qm = 1.7-1.8; associated with Fagaceae $ O. platyspora 3. 
8,9 $ Apothecia entire or split, up to 16 mm diam; spores narrowly ellipsoid to fusoid, Qm = 1.9-2.1; most likely associated with Betulaceae and Salicaceae
9,0 $ Apothecia deeply cup-shaped, split; ectal excipulum without resinous exudates $O. apophysata 1
9,0 $ Apothecia shallowly cup-shaped, usually entire; ectal excipulumsurface with abundant resinous exudates $ O. daliensis 2. 
10,11 $ Receptacle surface with bright citrine yellow tones in young apothecia
10,15 $ Receptacle surface without citrine yellow tones
11,12 $ Spores Qm = 1.7- 2; apothecial base at most wrinkled, without high ribs or veins
11,14 $ Spores Qm = 2-2.3; apothecial base with ribs or strongly veined at least in some apothecia
12,0 $ Receptacle surface ochraceous yellow; basal mycelium yellow in KOH; spores Wm = 6.5 µm , Qm = 1.7 $ O. borealis 25.
12,13 $ Receptacle surface citrine yellow; basal mycelium unchanged in KOH; spores Wm = 5.6-6 µm , Qm = 1.8-2
13,0 $ Apothecia sometimes entire, sometimes with blunt ribs at the base; North America $ O. oregonensis 31. 
13,0 $ Apothecia split, without ribs at the base; Europe $ O. concinna 27. 
14,0 $ Apothecia mostly entire, base strongly ribbed-veined and anastomosing in all the apothecia $ O. phlebophora 32. 
14,0 $ Apothecia mostly split, base with a few ribs-veins, smooth in some apothecia $ O. minor 30. 
15,16 $ At least some paraphyses straight or curved, claviform to almost capitate at apices; and/or resinous exudates of the ectal excipulum yellow or reddish yellow, turning bright yellow in KOH; apothecia split or not
15,20 $ Paraphyses not as such; resinous exudates of the ectal excipulum, when present, not turning bright yellow in KOH; apothecia always split
16,0 $ Apothecia shallowly cup-shaped and irregular, entire $O. lactea 29
16,17 $ . Apothecia ear-shaped or deeply cup-shaped, split . . . 17
17,18 $ Paraphyses often claviform or capitate at apices, 3-9 µm broad; spores Qm = 1.6-1.8
17,19 $ Paraphyses at most slightly swollen at apices, 2-5 µm broad; spores Qm = 1.9-2
18,0 $ Basal mycelium turning yellow in KOH; Europe $ (see under O. borealis) O. sp. 'a'
18,0 $ Basal mycelium not yellow in KOH; North America $ O. rainierensis 33. 
19,0 $ Spores Lm = 11.2-11.8 µm ; sometimes receptacle with a bluish hue, ochraceous grey when dried; basal tomentum light ochre in dried specimens $ O. caeruleopruinosa 26. 
19,0 $ Spores Lm = 10-10.6 µm ; receptacle without bluish hue when fresh, reddish brown when dried; basal tomentum orange-ochre in dried specimens $ O. flavidobrunneola 28. 
20,21 $ Spores Lm < 12 µm 
20,28 $ Spores Lm > 12 µm 
